# Route Table Reference

## 1. Public Route Table — VPC A

**Name:** `ewb-rt-a-public`

**Subnet association:** `ewb-a-public`

| Destination | Target | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `10.10.0.0/16` | Local | Communication inside VPC A |
| `0.0.0.0/0` | `ewb-igw-a` | Internet route |
| `10.20.0.0/16` | `ewb-a-b-peer` | Route to VPC B |

## 2. Private Route Table — VPC A

**Name:** `ewb-rt-a-private`

**Subnet association:** `ewb-a-private`

| Destination | Target | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `10.10.0.0/16` | Local | Communication inside VPC A |
| `10.20.0.0/16` | `ewb-a-b-peer` | Route to VPC B |

No default Internet Gateway route is configured for this private subnet.

## 3. Private Route Table — VPC B

**Name:** `ewb-rt-b-private`

**Subnet association:** `ewb-b-private`

| Destination | Target | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| `10.20.0.0/16` | Local | Communication inside VPC B |
| `10.10.0.0/16` | `ewb-a-b-peer` | Route to VPC A |

## Important

VPC Peering does not automatically configure route tables. The correct routes must be added to the route tables used by the communicating subnets.
